internet. In 2024, a team led by Ilia Shumailov published a study in
Nature showing what happens when models train, generation after
generation, on data produced by earlier models. Shumailov et al., AI
models collapse when trained on recursively generated data
Picture one simplified training lineage. Call the real human data dis-
tribution P0(x). Each new generation k learns from finite samples of
the one before:
Pk(x) = ∫qk(x|y)Pk − 1(y) dy
Here qk is a schematic transition kernel for the training and sampling
process,  not  the  paperʼs  general  equation  for  a  language  model.
Under the repeated resampling conditions the study examines, two
things  can  happen.  This  isnʼt  a  claim  that  every  use  of  synthetic
training data causes collapse; selection, checking, and access to real
data change the setup.
The  tails  get  pruned. Models  favor  high-probability  outputs  and
under-sample the rare ones. Over generations, the spread of the dis-
tribution shrinks. The weird hand-made engineering trick, the spe-
cific local history, the odd brilliant idea, those live in the tails, and
theyʼre the first to go.
The  errors  compound. A  modelʼs  small  biases  become  the  next
modelʼs training data, and the next. Some of the paperʼs simplified
examples converge toward a narrow spike. That spike neednʼt sit at
the original average; a distribution can lose its variety and drift at the
same time. A copy of a copy of a copy.
